[Music]  welcome back to cbs mornings there is  major news this morning in the  years-long fight for equal pay by the  women's national soccer team the team  and the u.s soccer federation have  reached a settlement for a total of 24  million dollars in their equal pay  discrimination lawsuit the players sued  the federation back in 2019 citing  unequal pay compared with their male  counterparts we're joined now by players  there's megan repino and alex morgan  hello along with cindy parlo cohn she's  a president of the u.s soccer federation  good morning one and all we're very  excited to have you here this morning i  want to start with the breaking news  alex in particular hello guys can you  put into words what it feels like this  morning  when we talk about you finally reach the  settlement after fighting this long and  standing up for yourselves put into  words what this feels like megan could  you start us off  oh i'll do my best but um it's a little  bit surreal to be honest um obviously  we've been in this for a long time and  um you know coming from a long history  of of women that have fought to put this  sport in a better place so i mean i  think pride comes to mind um just  incredibly proud of  the women on this team and all all the  women who this lawsuit represents  our greater extended team  obviously we have you know lawyers and  um strategists and media people to help  us um everybody's been in our corner the  entire time i feel like the thing i look  look forward to and i'm really proud of  is that you know the justice comes in  the next generation never having to go  through what we went through it's it's  equal pay across the board from here on  out and i'm looking forward to  continuing to grow the sport and to uh  have this be a moment we look back on uh  that signals a a new us soccer that we  can all be extremely proud of  alex the suit originally asked for 66  million dollars you settled for 24  million why did you all decide on that  amount and why now  uh we really got full back pay on  you know everything non-world cup  bonuses and i think  an important piece of it was equalizing  not only  the  money moving forward but the world cup  bonuses as well and working with u.s  soccer and the men's players association  to find a way to equalize that prize  money moving forward obviously we still  con fifa for that um but this is a huge  step and mending that relationship with  u.s soccer is also a big piece to that  and we felt we feel very  comfortable and happy and proud with the  moment that we've got to right now  because it is a huge win for  us for women's sports  for women in general  and it's a moment that we can all  celebrate right now  hey cindy it's tony here picking up on  what alex just said about mending the  relationship with u.s soccer what is the  state of that relationship after now  almost six years of fighting essentially  the women sued their employer  alleging discrimination uh so what is  the state of the relationship and also  what motivated u.s soccer to come back  to the negotiating table when you could  have kept pressing this in court  well you know as a former player myself  i have huge motivation to move past this  contentious litigation  and so i just want to move the sport  forward and like alex said this is a  huge win for everyone this is a win for  the players u.s soccer the sport women  in sports and just women in general so  for me it's about moving forward  together and really focusing on what's  important in growing the game both here  at home and across the world  cindy i want to i want to go right back  to you the settlement is contingent on  the new collective bargaining agreement  and as a former nfl player i understand  that aspect of it but what does that  look like when it comes to the contracts  does that mean that the bonuses will be  the same as the men's contracts  yeah so everything will be the same  structure  obviously each team can have a  difference because the teams have  different needs  but we're trying to do it in a very open  and transparent way inviting both sides  to the to each other's negotiations  so but moving forward everything will be  equal including fifa world cup prize  money  how will it affect you know the back  play the back pay for the other players  will there be backstage for other  players  okay well you know the back pay is  actually going to be within the statue  of limitation and for the players that  that played during that time period so  it might not  it might not be for the players who have  only joined recently  but that will be in legal terms but i  think the biggest win that we can look  at today is equalizing um is equal pay  moving forward and so yeah the back pay  is going to help some of us players who  have been on the team for a lot more  years but at the same time this is going  to benefit those younger players much  bigger can you talk in a little more  detail megan this might be best directed  to you about the benefits  for that next generation of talent so  the girls in the pipeline who want to  make the women's team  yeah i mean i think that the biggest  thing as an athlete is you always want  to have the ability to focus on your  sport with everything that you have it  takes a sort of mental emotional and  spiritual commitment uh to be a  professional athlete and you know as you  know we're we're up right now i think  it's 3 50 uh pacific time  we've spent countless hours on the  lawsuit or cba negotiations or whatever  it is  i think the the biggest um you know  piece of little legacy that that us us  of this generation can take is that the  players coming up next will have a much  better ability to just play and with  that comes  you know them probably being way better  than we ever were that's that's kind of  the goal um obviously equal pay moving  forward um not just from a base salary  perspective but from the bonuses as well  um this team has proven um time and  again to be incredibly successful on the  world stage on the biggest stage in the  world cups that's what everybody aspires  to and there'll be a much bigger prize  and pot of money going forward but i  think the working conditions as well  that's something that we settled a bit  ago but it's you know the types of uh  charter flights that we're taking it's  the hotels that we're staying in it's  our support staff it's everything a to z  the facilities the facilities does this  mean you'll be paid the same as the men  does this mean guys you'll be paid the  same as the men going forward  it does yes equal on all fronts um  including world cup bonuses  and megan i think you you you found what  is a joyous and unifying note to end on  there that this means that the women can  just play just play just play which is  ultimately what it's all about uh and i  think we should end on that note it's a  beautiful one this is a big day for all  of us for all of you megan rupino alex  morgan cindy parlo cohen thank you very  much for being here megan i love your  hair i love it thank you appreciate  y'all  all right bye you guys

